<% content_for :title, "#{@portfolio.user.display_name}'s portfolio" %>
<%# Student portfolio.

    Laid out as the standard dashboard shape rather than a two-column split: a KPI row, then the
    chart beside the earnings breakdown, then holdings full width. Robinhood, Fidelity, Wealthfront
    and Stripe all read top-down like this - the headline figures, then the trend, then the detail
    table - and it is what fixes the reported problem. Before, a six-column holdings table was
    squeezed into a two-thirds column while a narrow label/value list took the other third, so the
    widest content had the least room and the page had no alignment to read down.

    One gutter, 24px, everywhere. It used to be gap-8 between the columns, gap-6 inside them and
    gap-4 between the stats: three rhythms on one screen, which is most of what "does not line up"
    means. Every card is .tw-card now too - the stat cards had their own rounded-xl/shadow-xs, so
    there were four card treatments here.

    The KPI row decomposes the total rather than repeating it: value = cash + invested, which is a
    thing worth showing a student learning what a portfolio is. It also removes two duplications -
    cash was in a stat *and* in the earnings-to-invest card, and earnings were in a stat *and* in
    the breakdown card, which is why the page felt busy. Earnings now live only in the breakdown.

    The page's one primary action is in the header, which is where a page CTA belongs. It was the
    earnings-to-invest card's "Invest now"; that card stays on the trading floor, where the balance
    is context for shopping rather than a repeat of the stat above it. %>

<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto space-y-6">
  <%# The heading used to upcase the username and append a comma plus the word PORTFOLIO in
      capitals - shouty all-caps, which design.md rules out. It was built with .upcase, so no copy
      sweep could have found it. A username is a proper noun, so it keeps its own capitalisation. %>
  <%# A trail rather than a single back link, and rooted per role - see `PortfoliosController#breadcrumbs_for`.
      The back link that was here first said only "Back to Robin Fields"; the trail says that and the list
      above it, which is what a reader who came two levels in actually needs. It renders nothing at all for
      the owner, whose portfolio is a navbar item. %>
  <%= page_breadcrumbs(@breadcrumbs, root: current_user.admin? ? :admin : :app) %>

  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: "#{@portfolio.user.display_name}'s portfolio",
                       description: "Share values use the last closing price, updated once a day - " \
                                    "so this moves overnight, not while you are on the page." } do %>
    <%# **Rendered whatever the table holds.** This used to be suppressed while the holdings were empty,
        on the grounds that the empty state below carried the action - the two were the same destination
        twice on one screen, and that was the half that went. It is the other half now: an empty state
        explains, the page header acts, so the button is in one place in every state rather than moving
        when the first share lands. A student with nothing yet is exactly the reader who needs the route
        to the trading floor, and suppressing it here left them none once the empty state's went. %>
    <% if current_user.student? && @portfolio.trading_enabled? %>
      <%= link_to "Invest now", stocks_path, class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
    <% end %>

  <% end %>

  <% if @portfolio.student? && @portfolio.trading_off_notice? %>
    <%= render layout: "components/ui/callout",
               locals: { title: "Trading is turned off for your classroom",
                         body: "You cannot buy or sell right now. Your teacher can turn it back on." } do %>
      <%= render "portfolios/trading_off_dismiss", portfolio: @portfolio %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "portfolios/first_share" %>

  <%# Four equal cards, so the row reads as one band. Value, then the two things it is made of,
      then the count. %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-4">
    <%# The comparison is withheld in a student's first month, when there is no snapshot before
        this one to compare against - an honest absence rather than a change of zero. %>
    <%= render "components/ui/stat",
               label: "Portfolio value",
               value: number_to_currency(@portfolio.calculate_total_value),
               change: portfolio_change_label(@insights),
               change_up: @insights.change_up?,
               hint: "Your cash plus your shares",
               testid: "portfolio-value" %>

    <%= render "components/ui/stat",
               label: "Cash to invest",
               value: number_to_currency(@portfolio.cash_balance),
               hint: "Ready to spend on shares",
               testid: "cash-balance" %>

    <%= render "components/ui/stat",
               label: "Invested",
               value: number_to_currency(@portfolio.holdings_value),
               hint: "What your shares are worth today",
               testid: "holdings-value" %>

    <%= render "components/ui/stat",
               label: "Shares held",
               value: @portfolio.portfolio_stocks.sum(:shares).to_i,
               hint: "Across all companies",
               testid: "total-stocks" %>
  </div>

  <%# The chart takes two thirds and the breakdown one, which is the way round they want: a trend
      needs width, a label/value list does not. %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="lg:col-span-2">
      <%= render "portfolios/portfolio_chart" %>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:col-span-1">
      <%= render "shared/earnings_breakdown",
                 summary: @earnings_summary,
                 title: "Earnings breakdown",
                 subtitle: "How you earned your cash to invest",
                 class: "h-full" %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Two thirds and one third, the same rhythm as the chart row above: the summary is a sentence
      and wants width, the personal best is a stat and does not.

      This was three equal thirds, with a "Companies you own" card in the middle. That card is gone.
      It was a second list of the companies the holdings table below already lists, with the same
      logos - the duplication this page was rebuilt to remove - and it was a wall of unlabelled
      logos, which only works if you already recognise the brands. The logos are still there, in
      the table, now beside the company's name. Removing it also removed the reported dead space:
      grid children stretch to the tallest in the row, so its wrapping logo strip was padding out
      the bottom of its neighbours. %>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="lg:col-span-2"><%= render "portfolios/money_at_work" %></div>
    <div class="lg:col-span-1"><%= render "portfolios/best_month" %></div>
  </div>

  <%# Holdings, on the shared table system.

      Was a saturated amber header band inside a black/60 border with a
      black/40 rule under every row, and a min-w-[700px] that forced a
      horizontal scroll at any width. Money columns were left-aligned.

      Numeric columns are now right-aligned with tabular figures so they read
      down the column, and there is an empty state - previously an empty
      portfolio rendered a header with nothing under it. %>
  <%# **The actions column exists only if some row can act.** `Trade` is gated on the *viewer* being a
      student, not on anything about the row, so the column is all links or all dashes and never mixed -
      and a teacher or an admin got a trailing column of italic hyphens, one per holding.

      design.md already had the rule ("a column of dashes is not a column") and this page was written into
      `table_consistency_test` as its exception: "the dash convention stays where a column holds actions for
      some rows and not others - portfolios#show". That exemption was wrong about this table and nobody
      rendered it as a non-student to find out. The dash is right on `classrooms#index`, where the
      permission genuinely varies per row.

      One flag, read by the header, the cells and the empty state's colspan, because three places deciding
      the same thing separately is how a header outlives its cells. %>
  <% holdings_actions = current_user.student? %>
  <div data-testid="holdings-table">
    <%= render layout: "components/ui/data_table", locals: { caption: "Your current holdings" } do %>
      <%# The class belongs on the row, not the thead - it is the header row's border. Every
          other table in the app puts it there, and a computed-style sweep found this one
          carrying its separator one element up. %>
      <thead>
        <tr class="table-header-row">
          <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Company</th>
          <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden lg:table-cell">Qty.</th>
          <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden lg:table-cell">Last price</th>
          <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden lg:table-cell">Change</th>
          <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden lg:table-cell">Total return</th>
          <% if holdings_actions %>
            <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell hidden lg:table-cell"><span class="sr-only">Actions</span></th>
          <% end %>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% if @portfolio.positions.any? %>
          <% @portfolio.positions.each do |position| %>
            <% change_amount = position.change_amount %>
            <% total_return_amount = position.total_return_amount %>
            <% logo_path = "stock_logos/#{position.stock_ticker}-logo.svg" %>
            <%# Below lg this table is one column: the company, then its figures as labelled lines and
                the Trade action beneath them. Each figure is captured once so the two widths cannot
                diverge. Measured before: 101px of sideways scroll at 375px, which put Total return and
                Trade off the edge on the first screen a student sees. The sign stays inside the capture
                so direction is never carried by colour alone at either width. %>
            <% qty_cell = capture { share_count(position.shares) } %>
            <% price_cell = capture { number_to_currency(position.stock_current_price) } %>
            <% change_cell = capture do %>
              <span class="<%= change_amount >= 0 ? "text-green-700" : "text-red-700" %>">
                <%= change_amount >= 0 ? "+" : "" %><%= number_to_currency(change_amount) %>
              </span>
            <% end %>
            <% return_cell = capture do %>
              <span class="<%= total_return_amount >= 0 ? "text-green-700" : "text-red-700" %>">
                <%= total_return_amount >= 0 ? "+" : "" %><%= number_to_currency(total_return_amount) %>
              </span>
            <% end %>
            <% row_actions = capture do %>
              <% if holdings_actions %>
                <%= ghost_action_link "Trade", stocks_path, icon: "arrow-right-left" %>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>

            <tr class="table-body-row">
              <td class="table-body-cell">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                  <% if File.exist?("app/assets/images/#{logo_path}") %>
                    <%# alt="" is correct: the company name sits beside it, so a description
                        here would be read out twice. size-9 matches the icon tile token. %>
                    <%= image_tag asset_path(logo_path),
                                  class: "size-9 shrink-0 rounded-lg",
                                  alt: "",
                                  loading: "lazy" %>
                  <% end %>
                  <%# The standard three-part identity: logo, company name, ticker. It was the
                      ticker alone, so a student read "KO" with no way to know that is Coca-Cola -
                      the same clarity problem the logo-only collection card had. Every brokerage
                      list (Robinhood, Fidelity, Schwab) leads with the name and keeps the ticker
                      as the secondary line. design.md's primary-cell rule: body size, medium
                      weight, with a text-xs slate-600 line beneath. %>
                  <div class="flex flex-col">
                    <span class="font-medium text-slate-900"><%= position.stock.company_name %></span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-slate-600"><%= position.stock_ticker %></span>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <%= render "components/ui/stacked_row_fields",
                           fields: [["Qty.", qty_cell], ["Last price", price_cell],
                                    ["Change", change_cell], ["Total return", return_cell]],
                           actions: row_actions %>
              </td>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden whitespace-nowrap lg:table-cell"><%= qty_cell %></td>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden whitespace-nowrap lg:table-cell"><%= price_cell %></td>

              <%# A sign as well as a colour, so the direction is not carried by
                  colour alone. green-700/red-700 clear AA where 600 does not. %>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden whitespace-nowrap lg:table-cell"><%= change_cell %></td>
              <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden whitespace-nowrap lg:table-cell"><%= return_cell %></td>

              <%# A row action, so it is the ghost, like every other row action in the app. It was a filled
                  primary repeated down every holding - the shape design.md rules out for a per-row action.

                  No dash branch: the cell is not rendered at all for a viewer who cannot trade, because
                  then *no* row can and a column of hyphens says nothing. See the note on the flag above.

                  arrow-right-left, not arrow-up-down. A vertical two-arrow glyph is the *sort* icon -
                  Material's swap_vert, and this app's own sort_icon renders exactly that as an unsorted
                  column's caret - so a vertical pair as a row action means "sort" in the one context where
                  sorting is a thing you do to rows. Horizontal is the exchange glyph: Material's
                  swap_horiz, and what TD, Chase, Monzo and Revolut all use for a transfer. %>
              <% if holdings_actions %>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden table-actions-cell lg:table-cell">
                  <div class="flex items-center justify-end gap-1">
                    <%= ghost_action_link "Trade", stocks_path, icon: "arrow-right-left" %>
                  </div>
                </td>
              <% end %>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <tr class="table-body-row">
            <td colspan="<%= holdings_actions ? 6 : 5 %>" class="p-0">
              <%# Leads with the student's own balance: this is the first screen every student
                  sees, and "you have $24.00 ready" is about them in a way "no holdings yet" is
                  not. "You can buy a single share" removes the thing that stops people starting.
                  Someone viewing another student's portfolio gets the plain version - it is not
                  their balance to be invited to spend. %>
              <% if current_user.student? %>
                <%= render layout: "components/ui/empty_state",
                           locals: { icon: "sparkles",
                                     title: "You have #{number_to_currency(@portfolio.cash_balance)} ready to invest",
                                     body: "Pick a company you know - you can buy a single share, and sell " \
                                           "whenever you like. Shares you buy appear here." } do %><% end %>
              <% else %>
                <%= render layout: "components/ui/empty_state",
                           locals: { icon: "chart-line",
                                     title: "No holdings yet",
                                     body: "Shares bought on the trading floor appear here." } do %>
                <% end %>
              <% end %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
